\section{Two ways a quantity can be absent, and why they differ}{
rnetis has two distinct responses to a quantity a fit does not have, and the
difference is deliberate rather than inconsistent:
\itemize{
\item \strong{This function returns an empty frame with the documented columns} and a
\code{reason} attribute. Variance components are a \emph{component} of a result:
asking for them is reasonable on any fit, the answer is legitimately "there
are none", and downstream code binding several fits' components together
should not have to special-case the ones without. An empty frame of the
right shape composes; an error does not.
\item \strong{\code{\link[=pseudo_r2]{pseudo_r2()}} errors instead.} There, the absence is the whole answer to
the question asked — a caller asking for variance explained by a fit that
has no agreed decomposition is asking something with no correct return
value, and a zero-row frame would read as "explained nothing."
}

Both give an absence rather than a fabricated value, and neither returns a table
of \code{NA}s.
}

\section{What \code{residual_variance_share} is, and when it is absent}{


It is a \strong{conditional} variance share: of the variance left unexplained
\emph{after} conditioning on this model's predictors, the proportion at each
level. It is \strong{not} the unconditional ICC(1) of the aggregation literature
(Bliese 2000; Kozlowski & Klein 2000) — that is \code{\link[=icc1]{icc1()}}, computed from a
null model with no predictors, and it answers a different question. The two
can differ substantially, and the comparison is informative: a large
\code{\link[=icc1]{icc1()}} beside a small \code{residual_variance_share} says the predictors
account for much of the original between-group heterogeneity.

\strong{It is \code{NA} for every row when the model has a random slope}, with the
reason recorded in the returned object's \code{icc_reason} attribute. Between-
group variance is then \code{tau00 + 2*x*tau01 + x^2*tau11} — a function of the
slope predictor — so no scalar partitions the variance at any row. The
variance and covariance estimates themselves are still reported; only the
share is withheld, because there is no single value it could take.

For a binary (probit) Selection fit the dyadic residual variance is fixed
at 1 by the identification constraint, not estimated. It still enters the
share denominator, because it is genuinely part of the total variance the
shares are shares of -- but a reader should not interpret it as a finding.
}

\section{Selection fits}{
 shares of one dyad's latent variance:

This decomposition of a dyad's latent variance is this package's own
construction, not a published statistic. For a Selection fit the share is each
component's contribution to the latent variance of a single dyad (i, j),
conditional on the predictors:
\code{va + vb + V_uv + s2} for a directed relation and \verb{2 va + V_uv + s2} for a
symmetric one, where \code{V_uv} is the variance of the multiplicative term.
There is \strong{no \verb{2 cab} term}: \code{a_i} and \code{b_j} belong to different actors
and are independent. The sender-receiver covariance \code{cab} and the
within-dyad correlation \code{rho} are reported as rows but carry no share.

With \code{R > 0} a \verb{multiplicative (latent-factor) variance} row is added and
enters the denominator. It is
the variance of the posterior-mean product, which understates it; the
returned frame then carries a \code{multiplicative_note} attribute saying so.
On a symmetric fit the actor variance row's share is \verb{2 va} over the total,
because each dyad involves two independently drawn actors.

A hierarchical multi-group Selection fit (\code{pooling = selection_pooling()})
is refused with an error naming \code{\link[=netis_hierarchy]{netis_hierarchy()}} and the fit's
\code{convergence_table}, which carry its between-group and within-network
quantities.
}

